4. Critical Interview Topics (2026)

Q1: Design a CI/CD Pipeline for ML Models
Key talking points:
- Data versioning (DVC) is as important as code versioning
- Model registry tracks lineage: data + code + hyperparameters
- Canary deployment: Route 5% traffic to new model, auto-rollback if metrics degrade
- Shadow deployment: Run new model in parallel, log predictions but serve old model's predictions

Q2: How Do You Monitor Models in Production?
Key talking points:
- "The most dangerous drift is silent drift — high confidence but wrong outputs"
- Set up two types of alerts: statistical (distribution shifted >X%) and business (conversion dropped >Y%)
- Monitor: feature distributions, prediction distributions, latency (p50, p95, p99), error rates, business metrics

Q3: Explain Model Serving Infrastructure
Stack to know:

```
API Gateway → Load Balancer → Serving Framework (vLLM/TGI/TensorRT-LLM) 
→ GPU Inference → Response Streaming
```

Auto-scaling strategy:
- Scale on GPU utilization + request queue depth (not CPU)
- Scale-up: queue depth > threshold for >30 seconds
- Scale-down: GPU utilization <20% for >5 minutes
- Minimum replicas: Always keep 1+ warm (cold start = 30-120 seconds)

Q4: Quantization for LLM Deployment
- INT8: Nearly lossless, sweet spot for production
- INT4: Degrades quality 1-3% but halves memory
- FP8 (E4M3/E5M2): Emerging standard on H100s with native hardware support
- AWQ > GPTQ in most benchmarks (protects salient weights)

Q5: Continuous Batching for LLM Serving
- Static Batching: All requests wait for longest request → GPU idle 50-70% of time
- Continuous Batching: New request takes slot as soon as one finishes → GPU utilization 80-95%, 2-3x throughput

